          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong model category
            (sometimes called a
            %em Quillen model category
            or a
            = succeed "," do
              %em closed model category
            %strong not
            related to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"}> closed category
            ”) is a context for doing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complexes
          %p
            So, what is a model category? For starters, it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            equipped with three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            and called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The weak equivalences play the role of ‘
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"}> homotopy equivalences
                ’ or something a bit more general (such as
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
                if there is a map from one to the other that induces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
                for any choice of basepoint in the first space. These maps are more general than homotopy equivalences, so they are called ‘weak equivalences’.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
                play the role of ‘nice surjections’. For example, in the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                with its usual
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} Quillen model structure on topological spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
                is a fibration. More generally the fibrations here are the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibrations"} Serre fibrations
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
                play the role of ‘nice inclusions’. For example, in the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                with its usual
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} model structure on topological spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NDR+pair"} NDR pair
                is typically a cofibration.
          %p
            A bit more technically: we can define an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            starting from any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in our original category,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            between objects,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            between morphisms, homotopies between homotopies, and so on,
            = succeed "." do
              %em ad infinitum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            of the underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+nerve"} homotopy coherent nerve
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicial subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi cf
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{cf}\subset M
            of cofibrant-fibrant objects. We say this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            is
            %em presented
            (or modeled) by the model category, and that the objects of the model category are
            %em models
            for the objects of this
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limits"} homotopy limits
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
          %p
            In this sense model categories are ‘models for
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            Recall that the idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
            is to work just with 1-morphisms instead of with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-morphisms"} n-morphisms
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalences
            in the full
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            in the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
          %p
            In a model category the data of weak equivalences is accompanied by further auxiliary data that helps to compute the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categorical+hom-space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+a+model+category"} homotopy category of a model category
            for more on that.
          %p
            If the model category happens to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+simplicial+model+category"} combinatorial simplicial model category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}
            it
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presents
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                    %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}^\circ
            in the form of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
            given by the full
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SSet"} SSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched subcategory
            on objects that are both fibrant and cofibrant.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The following is a somewhat terse account. For a more detailed exposition see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory"} Introduction to Homotopy Theory
            the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory#ModelCategoryTheory"} Abstract homotopy theory
          %p
            %br/
          #ModelStructure.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong model structure
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a choice of three distinguished
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %em cofibrations
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Cof
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi Mor
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cof \subset Mor(\mathcal{C})
              %li
                %p
                  %em fibrations
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Fib
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi Mor
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fib \subset Mor(\mathcal{C})
              %li
                %p
                  %em weak equivalences
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi Mor
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subset Mor(\mathcal{C})
            %p satisfying the following conditions:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  makes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  into a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
                %p
                  (meaning that it contains all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                  and is closed under
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-three"} two-out-of-three
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g, g f
                  are in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Cof
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Fib
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Cof, Fib \cap W)
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Cof
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi W
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Fib
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Cof \cap W, Fib)
                  are two
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
                  on
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          #ModelCategory.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong model category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              equipped with a model structure according to def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModelStructure"}
          %p
            This equivalent version of the definition was observed in (
            %a{:href => "#Joyal"}> Joyal, def. E.1.2
            ), highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#Riehl09"}> Riehl 09
            ). This definition already implies all the closure conditions on classes of morphisms which other definitions in the literature explicitly ask for, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ClosureOfMorphisms"} below
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              %strong (terminology)
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The morphisms in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi Fib
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Fib
                  (the fibrations that are also weak equivalences) are called
                  %strong trivial fibrations
                  or
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+fibrations"} acyclic fibrations
              %li
                %p
                  The morphisms in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi Cof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Cof
                  (the cofibrations that are also weak equivalences) are called
                  %strong trivial cofibrations
                  or
                  = succeed "." do
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+cofibrations"} acyclic cofibrations
              %li
                %p
                  An object is called
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant"} cofibrant
                  if the unique morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \to X
                  from the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
                  is a cofibration
              %li
                %p
                  An object is called
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant"} fibrant
                  if the unique morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to *
                  to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                  is a fibration.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p Often, the fibrant and cofibrant objects are the ones one is “really” interested in, but the category consisting only of these is not well-behaved (as a 1-category). The factorizations supply fibrant and cofibrant replacement functors which allow us to treat any object of the model category as a ‘model’ for its fibrant-cofibrant replacement.
          %h2#variants Variants
          %h3#slight_variations_on_the_axioms Slight variations on the axioms
          %p
            Quillen’s original definition required only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+colimits"} finite colimits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DwyerHirschhornKan97"} Dwyer, Hirschhorn &amp; Kan 1997
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DwyerHirschhornKanSmith2004"} Dwyer, Hirschhorn, Kan &amp; Smith 2004
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+W.+Thomason"} Robert W. Thomason
            proposed to require that the factorizations given by (ii) are actually
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+factorization+systems"} functorial factorization systems
          %p
            resulting in the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+category"} Thomason model category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Hovey"} Mark Hovey
            later included the data of such a functorial factorization (and not just its existence) into his definition of a model category. In practice, Quillen’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
            means that many model categories can be made to have functorial factorizations. (But not all: an example of a model category with non-functorial factorizations can be found in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0106152"} Isaksen 2001
          %h3#enhancements_of_the_axioms Enhancements of the axioms
          %p There are several extra conditions that strengthen the notion of a model category:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+model+category"} monoidal model category
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                that is also a model category in a compatible way.
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+model+category"} enriched model category
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
                over a monoidal category, that is also a model category in a compatible way.
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+model+category"} algebraic model category
                is one where the two defining
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
                are refined to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+weak+factorization+systems"} algebraic weak factorization systems
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrantly+generated+model+category"} cofibrantly generated model category
                is one with a good compatible notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+complexes"} cell complexes
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
                is a cofibrantly generated one that in addition is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+model+category"} accessible model category
                is one on a locally presentable category that admits
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+functor"} accessible
                factorizations, which can therefore be enhanced to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+weak+factorization+systems"} algebraic weak factorization systems
            %li
              %p
                A left/right
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} proper model category
                is one where the weak equivalences are stable under pushforward along cofibrations / pullback along fibrations
          %h3#weaker_axiom_systems Weaker axiom systems
          %p
            There are several notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            with similar but less structure than a full model category.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
                has a notion of just weak equivalences and fibrations, none of cofibrations. As the name implies, all of its objects are fibrant; the canonical example is the subcategory of fibrant objects in a model category.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+category"} Waldhausen category
                dually has a notion of weak equivalences and cofibrations, and all of its objects are cofibrant.
            %li
              %p
                There is also a slight variant of the full notion of model category by Thomason that is designed to make the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+model+structure+on+functors"} global model structure on functors
                more naturally accessible: this is the notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+category"} Thomason model category
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+path+objects"} category with path objects
                is similar to a category of fibrant objects but has
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+objects"} path space objects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semimodel+category"} Semimodel categories
                relax some of the conditions on lifting properties.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+model+category"} Weak model categories
                relax these conditions even further.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premodel+category"} Premodel categories
                are an even weaker notion that allows for a nice 2-categorical treatment.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#ClosureOfMorphisms Closure of morphism classes under retracts
          %p
            As a consequence of the definition, the classes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cof
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Fib
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cof, Fib
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            each
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                are closed under
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Arr
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Arr C
            %li
              %p
                are closed under
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            %li
              %p
                contain all the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cof
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fib
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fib
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi Cof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Cof
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi Fib
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Fib
            this and further closure properties are discussed in detail at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
            in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "weak+factorization+system#ClosureProperties"} Closure properties
          %p
            In the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+factorizations"} functorial factorizations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is closed under retracts: for any retract diagram may then be funtorially factored with the middle morphism factored through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi Cof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Cof
            followed by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi Fib
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap Fib
          %p Without assuming functorial factorization the statement still holds:
          #WeakEquivalencesAreClosedUnderRetracts.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Given a model category in the sense of def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModelCategory"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
              (in the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Joyal"}> Joyal, prop. E.1.3
            ), highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#Riehl09"}> Riehl 09
            )
          .proof
